#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087404 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff002e26-f100-5b44-9175-a363d0808f9d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Single Point of Intersection of a Circle with a Line"}] :question/id #uuid "628a6c4d-0185-5576-8b8f-b217cdf3c76b" :question/math-academy-id "e-3234" :question/problem "Find the point where the line $y = -x + 3$ intersects the circle $(x+3)^2 + (y-2)^2 = 8.$" :question/worked-solution "To find $x$-coordinate of the intersection point, we need to substitute $y = -x + 3$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =8 \\\\ (x+3)^{2}+((-x+3)-2)^{2} & =8 \\\\ (x+3)^{2}+(-x+1)^{2} & =8 \\\\ {x}^{2}+6x+9+{x}^{2}-2x+1 & =8 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+4x+10 & =8 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x+5 & =4 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x+1 & =0 \\\\ (x+1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x+1 & =0 \\\\ x & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $y$-coordinate of the intersection point, we need to plug in $x = -1$ into the equation of the line and compute $y$-value as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-x+3 \\\\  & =-(-1)+3 \\\\  & =1+3 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the line $y = -x + 3$ intersects the circle $(x+3)^2 + (y-2)^2 = 8$ at the point $(-1,4).$\n\nA sketch of the circle and the line is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/90f7e5f187ad79e18d36ce253c8f1f998bc5a92790909f4840c84789c2ae5222.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087405 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d0e48e3-39ca-551a-80a4-dc7ff7855197" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying When a Circle Does Not Intersect with a Line"}] :question/id #uuid "e4045e5f-331f-5b16-8a4e-681c5802792d" :question/math-academy-id "e-3235" :question/problem "Find the points where the circle $(x-2)^2 + (y+1)^2 = 3$ intersects the line $y = 3x - 1.$" :question/worked-solution "To find $x$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute $y = 3x - 1$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x$, if possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+(y+1)^{2} & =3 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+((3x-1)+1)^{2} & =3 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+(3x)^{2} & =3 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x+4+9{x}^{2} & =3 \\\\ 10{x}^{2}-4x+1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we compute the discriminant $\\mathcal{D}$ of the above quadratic equation, then we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =(-4)^{2}-4\\cdot 10\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =16-40 \\\\  & =-24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathcal{D} = -24 < 0$, there are no real solutions to the equation $10x^2 - 4x + 1 = 0.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the circle $(x-2)^2 + (y+1)^2 = 3$ does not intercept the line $y = 3x - 1$ at any point.\n\nThe corresponding diagram is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/590fa0a3fb8cb0e80c46859ad19b9457528c40f79f1ba33edea217d8500b64de.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087406 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "603badf5-1f56-581f-833a-ec08fbedf777" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Circle Given a Line Covering a Diameter"}] :question/id #uuid "a2e23685-1ef7-588f-a4b8-5ab6abc60ee2" :question/math-academy-id "e-3236" :question/problem "The line $-4x+3y=24$ intersects the $x$- and $y$-axes at points $A$ and $B$, respectively. If the segment $\\overline{AB}$ is a diameter, find the equation of the circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/c8ed2363f6899e90eff46d8e45107ed5eee19582aeaed624c9831e5a128ff10a.png)" :question/worked-solution "To find the $x$-coordinate of $A$, we set $y=0$ and solve $-4x+3y=24$ for $x$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4x+3(0) & =24 \\\\ -4x & =24 \\\\ x & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, to find the $y$-coordinate of $B$, we set $x=0$ and solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4(0)+3y & =24 \\\\ 3y & =24 \\\\ y & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $A$ and $B$ have coordinates $(-6,0)$ and $(0,8)$, respectively.\n\nIf the segment $\\overline{AB}$ is a diameter of the circle, then the midpoint must be the center of the circle. Using the midpoint formula, we find that the center of the circle is at\n\n$$\n\\left( \\dfrac{-6+0}{2}, \\dfrac{0+8}{2}\\right) = (-3,4).\n$$\n\nNow, to find the radius of the circle, we compute the distance between the center $(-3,4)$ and a point on the circle, say $B(0,8),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{(0-(-3))^{2}+(8-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the circle has center $(-3,4)$ and radius $r=5,$ the equation of the circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-(-3))^{2}+(y-4)^{2} & ={5}^{2} \\\\ (x+3)^{2}+(y-4)^{2} & =25.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087549 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e44305e5-b7e3-5ae2-925c-9329f37823b8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points of Intersection of a Circle with a Line"}] :question/id #uuid "a3ff8836-708c-558f-82b1-49adac6b0ed6" :question/math-academy-id "e-6761" :question/problem "Find the points where the line $y = x +5$ intersects the circle $(x+2)^2 + y^2 = 9.$" :question/worked-solution "To find $x$-coordinate of the intersection points, we need to substitute $y = x + 5$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =9 \\\\ (x+2)^{2}+(x+5)^{2} & =9 \\\\ {x}^{2}+4x+4+{x}^{2}+10x+25 & =9 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+14x+29 & =9 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+14x+20 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+7x+10 & =0 \\\\ (x+2)(x+5) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute each of $x = -2$ and $x=-5$ into the equation of the line and compute $y$-value.\n\n- Substituting $x=-2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x+5 \\\\  & =-2+5 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to an intersection point $(-2,3).$\n\n- Substituting $x=-5,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x+5 \\\\  & =-5+5 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to an intersection point $(-5,0).$\n\nTherefore, the line $y = x + 5$ intersects the circle $(x+2)^2 + y^2 = 9$ at the points $(-2,3)$ and $(-5,0).$\n\nA sketch of the circle and the line is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/9298e1af177eb9ba1702af0053c03d1d0e696819bae717c4892efdcd574c78fc.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329055 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e44305e5-b7e3-5ae2-925c-9329f37823b8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points of Intersection of a Circle with a Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ee5a54a-bfba-5cfa-b0a4-267b6ecd3c54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,2)$ and $(-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{5}{6})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329057} {:answer/id #uuid "85826fff-b2b3-5574-871e-fcc02e875021" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(3,\\frac{7}{3})$ and $(-1,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329058} {:answer/id #uuid "549f7dc4-8b52-5bca-9629-1e67e6233db3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{5}{6})$ and $(-\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{5}{4})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329059} {:answer/id #uuid "d90d92ef-71e0-5265-aa9c-ceff1cb328d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,2)$ and $(-1,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329060} {:answer/id #uuid "d3a566e7-ffa5-5ec6-9fc0-2a252b40996d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(3,\\frac{7}{3})$ and $(-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{5}{6})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329061}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d90d92ef-71e0-5265-aa9c-ceff1cb328d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,2)$ and $(-1,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329060} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ececc18-1ac1-5cc4-865d-259fc6da1ed1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329056}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f9516424-4bd5-5885-82ea-4588d28a4cdd" :question/math-academy-id "q-117247" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/5180dfe04f31f43fdcdfa8bd720951df0c6fb0d73cd2db3912b37e172f714131.png)\n\nFind the points where the circle $(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2}=5$ intersects the line $x-3y=-4$ (sketched above)." :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation $x-3y=-4,$ as follows:\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{x}{3}+\\frac{4}{3}.\n$$\n\nTo find $x$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute $y=\\frac{x}{3}+\\frac{4}{3}$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2}=5 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+{(\\frac{x}{3}+\\frac{4}{3})}^{2} & =5 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{8x}{9}+\\frac{16}{9} & =5 \\\\ \\frac{10{x}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{10x}{9}+\\frac{25}{9} & =\\frac{45}{9} \\\\ \\frac{10{x}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{10x}{9}-\\frac{20}{9} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-x-2 & =0 \\\\ (x+1)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-1,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute $x=-1,2$ into the equation of the line and compute $y$-values. Start with $x=-1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{x}{3}+\\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first intersection point is $(-1,1).$ Next, we plug in $x=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{x}{3}+\\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second intersection point is $(2,2).$\n\nTherefore, the line $x-3y=-4$ intersects the circle $(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2}=5$ at the points $(2,2)$ and $(-1,1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329062 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff002e26-f100-5b44-9175-a363d0808f9d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Single Point of Intersection of a Circle with a Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b73fd24-5d1d-549f-ab0b-97f073f6002e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329064} {:answer/id #uuid "7dfc237b-0391-53f4-9e07-18741e9ef4be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329065} {:answer/id #uuid "92277082-f9fa-5699-9813-eeb338f3ff98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1.5,-6.5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329066} {:answer/id #uuid "092f5a5c-6a4c-5ad7-b107-25c85dafb8db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2.5,-3.5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329067} {:answer/id #uuid "f1be655d-1d80-5110-9c7d-43bb4301d17d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329068}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b73fd24-5d1d-549f-ab0b-97f073f6002e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329064} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f96ad68-0d47-56ff-a4ec-bb8b1a61ea5d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329063}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "67a9b2cd-1787-5032-a8c9-13cecd77788f" :question/math-academy-id "q-117542" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/a3a9751a19c9a853c425b81ce63ccb76b4cd4350f152df3dbe00a1cdb0f8cdab.png)\n\nFind the point where the line $y=-3x-11$ intersects the circle ${x}^{2}+(y+1)^{2}=10.$" :question/worked-solution "To find $x$-coordinate of the intersection point, we need to substitute $y=-3x-11$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y+1)^{2} & =10 \\\\ {x}^{2}+(-3x-11+1)^{2} & =10 \\\\ {x}^{2}+(-3x-10)^{2} & =10 \\\\ {x}^{2}+9{x}^{2}+60x+100 & =10 \\\\ 10{x}^{2}+60x+100 & =10 \\\\ 10{x}^{2}+60x+90 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+6x+9 & =0 \\\\ (x+3)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x+3 & =0 \\\\ x & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $y$-coordinate of the intersection point, we need to substitute $x=-3$ into the equation of the line and compute $y$-value, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-3x-11 \\\\  & =-3(-3)-11 \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the line $y=-3x-11$ intersects the circle ${x}^{2}+(y+1)^{2}=10$ at the point $(-3,-2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329069 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff002e26-f100-5b44-9175-a363d0808f9d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Single Point of Intersection of a Circle with a Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "73641b57-7ccc-5781-bbb1-059986ebf422" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329071} {:answer/id #uuid "2a9df10d-0406-5afa-8406-2bfc116d2091" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329072} {:answer/id #uuid "3803a30f-e30d-5793-bbc5-96e08b0ae9df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329073} {:answer/id #uuid "6307667c-c334-55b8-8947-075a4896981c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329074} {:answer/id #uuid "c24e1bac-3c95-597a-9c23-651cb8ce5d6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329075}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3803a30f-e30d-5793-bbc5-96e08b0ae9df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329073} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a61f814-3085-5e3e-982f-b82601f07526" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329070}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "90e9bd05-dbac-514e-ba2a-1c98522457da" :question/math-academy-id "q-117546" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/91ead412d3e333fd4bfbcce785813a530e66ca39858ca108e9920d349443bb53.png)\n\nFind the $x$-coordinate of the point where the line $-2x+3y=12$ intersects the circle $(x+1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}=13$ (sketched above)." :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation $-2x+3y=12,$ as follows:\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2x}{3}+4\n$$\n\nTo find $x$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute $y=\\frac{2x}{3}+4$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2} & =13 \\\\ (x+1)^{2}+{(\\frac{2x}{3}+4+1)}^{2} & =13 \\\\ (x+1)^{2}+{(\\frac{2x}{3}+5)}^{2} & =13 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x+1+\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{20x}{3}+25 & =13 \\\\ \\frac{13{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{26x}{3}+13 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+6x+9 & =0 \\\\ (x+3)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x+3 & =0 \\\\ x & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329076 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d0e48e3-39ca-551a-80a4-dc7ff7855197" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying When a Circle Does Not Intersect with a Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82379688-2b6e-5b92-8d47-74542f278bd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-3$ and $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329078} {:answer/id #uuid "f55058c9-58fd-5b86-8bfa-1216f657b136" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329079} {:answer/id #uuid "457da568-a1b7-5aef-bc5c-199e6b40d3a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-1$ and $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329080} {:answer/id #uuid "fd682f0b-f0a5-5698-831b-22adcc338263" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The circle and the line do not intersect" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329081} {:answer/id #uuid "933645e4-7426-5e55-85c4-26bb808dab00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-2$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329082}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd682f0b-f0a5-5698-831b-22adcc338263" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The circle and the line do not intersect" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329081} :answer-field/id #uuid "13942aed-5583-5808-af56-0712bdc58712" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329077}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ea101bc1-4f0a-50d4-b42a-0a6309938246" :question/math-academy-id "q-117734" :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinates of the points where the circle $(x+1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}=1$ intersects the line $y=-3x.$" :question/worked-solution "To find $x$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute $y=-3x$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x,$ if possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2} & =1 \\\\ (x+1)^{2}+(-3x+1)^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x+1+9{x}^{2}-6x+1 & =1 \\\\ 10{x}^{2}-4x+1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we compute the discriminant $D$ of the above quadratic equation, then we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =(-4)^{2}-4\\cdot 10\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =16-40 \\\\  & =-24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $D=-24<0$, there are no real solutions to the equation $10{x}^{2}-4x+1=0.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the circle $(x+1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}=1$ does not intersect the line $y=-3x.$ \n\nThe corresponding diagram is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/87f75953dbe795a8e5db201778b4ff3127f02bae30ef3b8e8ffa7bd7956c1bc3.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329083 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d0e48e3-39ca-551a-80a4-dc7ff7855197" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying When a Circle Does Not Intersect with a Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aaddf264-1d25-5a7b-b2be-f5563dc7bad2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329085} {:answer/id #uuid "1d1a878c-7f2f-5c35-bf28-7b0cf8af73a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329086} {:answer/id #uuid "10fefbb7-794d-58e2-a2c3-611ff4449143" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-1$ and $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329087} {:answer/id #uuid "65de35fb-0f08-5436-9a3c-c6f3c99f492f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The circle and the line do not intersect" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329088} {:answer/id #uuid "1d4549cb-c476-521e-9ec0-1fdcd890b7eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-2$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329089}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65de35fb-0f08-5436-9a3c-c6f3c99f492f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The circle and the line do not intersect" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329088} :answer-field/id #uuid "2963a972-c781-52d2-a755-4aa69c211d09" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329084}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "61ffdc01-8414-55ad-b1b4-42608bbe9957" :question/math-academy-id "q-117737" :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinates of the points where the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ intersects the line $x+2y=4$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation $x+2y=4,$ as follows:\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{2}x+2\n$$\n\nTo find $x$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute $y=-\\frac{1}{2}x+2$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x,$ if possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{(-\\frac{1}{2}x+2)}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-2x+4 & =1 \\\\ \\frac{5{x}^{2}}{4}-2x+3 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we compute the discriminant $D$ of the above quadratic equation, then we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={2}^{2}-4\\cdot \\frac{5}{4}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =4-15 \\\\  & =-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $D=-11<0$, there are no real solutions to the equation $\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{4}-2x+3=0.$\n\nHence, we conclude that the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ does not intersect the line $x+2y=4$ at any point.\n\nThe corresponding diagram is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/400b997f9f1e83d6d2dd6c34b68a01ad4de5b352e40de2401eb516ca181f3163.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329090 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "603badf5-1f56-581f-833a-ec08fbedf777" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Circle Given a Line Covering a Diameter"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "165c41bd-ae4e-5a9e-b357-7a44063862ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-6)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329092} {:answer/id #uuid "857b251c-9e0d-5097-b424-f99ecc32cbdd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-6)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329093} {:answer/id #uuid "30aa4d61-dbef-5ea3-805d-e03d06a6fe07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329094} {:answer/id #uuid "85ec250e-80f3-506a-b8ee-8fe0c0de78c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329095} {:answer/id #uuid "924b6a3a-5195-5a29-91f4-28c623f33f25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=2\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329096}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "165c41bd-ae4e-5a9e-b357-7a44063862ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-6)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329092} :answer-field/id #uuid "50bc64f8-b568-5737-bf3a-4901e1a75286" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329091}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2c7a8305-48d6-5002-af81-74f86e2adf20" :question/math-academy-id "q-117851" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/0e5d6c9ce3198b5950c8a5396cf5500f4bddd232fbe44613611873c294dda6ea.png)\n\nThe line $3y+x=12$ intercepts the $x$- and $y$-axes at points $A$ and $B$, respectively. If the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is a diameter of a circle, find the equation of the circle." :question/worked-solution "To find the $x$-coordinate of $A$, we set $y=0$ in the equation of the line and solve for $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(0)+x & =12 \\\\ x & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, to find the $y$-coordinate of $B$, we set $x=0$ and solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3y-0 & =12 \\\\ 3y & =12 \\\\ y & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $A$ and $B$ have the coordinates $(12,0)$ and $(0,4)$, respectively.\n\nIf the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is a diameter of the circle, then the midpoint must be the center of the circle. Using the midpoint formula, we find that the center of the circle is at\n\n$$\n(\\frac{12+0}{2},\\frac{0+4}{2})=(6,2).\n$$\n\nNow, to find the radius of the circle, we compute the distance between the center $(6,2)$ and a point on the circle, say $A(12,0),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{(6-12)}^{2}+(2-0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-6)}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{40} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, since the circle has center $(6,2)$ and radius $r=2\\sqrt{10},$ the equation of the circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x-6)}^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & ={(2\\sqrt{10})}^{2} \\\\ (x-6)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =40.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329097 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "603badf5-1f56-581f-833a-ec08fbedf777" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Circle Given a Line Covering a Diameter"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59548f33-3e11-584e-ad31-7a7410835c2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+8)^{2}+(y+2)^{2}=17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329099} {:answer/id #uuid "36d03c1e-0297-5df9-be70-7a899b50d645" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+4)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}=68" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329100} {:answer/id #uuid "f34bb61f-428d-52ba-87f5-f2806044c96c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+4)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}=17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329101} {:answer/id #uuid "d5755761-db97-5922-a440-b069e501e002" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+4)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}=2\\sqrt{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329102} {:answer/id #uuid "ec1b8649-14f1-5ce5-821e-a2e725b06541" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+8)^{2}+(y+2)^{2}=68" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329103}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f34bb61f-428d-52ba-87f5-f2806044c96c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+4)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}=17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329101} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7802fa3-d135-5961-8d50-b8a9a452ee00" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329098}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "910a6090-0381-577c-8b6e-114eac566776" :question/math-academy-id "q-117857" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/51d811f0b628a1854542d85954c26ca4658602cfcf7c207fbcfdb41115933139.png)\n\nThe line $4y+x=-8$ intercepts the $x$- and $y$-axes at points $A$ and $B$, respectively. If the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is a diameter of a circle, find the equation of the circle." :question/worked-solution "To find the $x$-coordinate of $A$, we set $y=0$ in the equation of the line and solve for $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(0)+x & =-8 \\\\ x & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, to find the $y$-coordinate of $B$, we set $x=0$ and solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4y-0 & =-8 \\\\ 4y & =-8 \\\\ y & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $A$ and $B$ have the coordinates $(-8,0)$ and $(0,-2)$, respectively.\n\nIf the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is a diameter of the circle, then the midpoint must be the center of the circle. Using the midpoint formula, we find that the center of the circle is at\n\n$$\n(\\frac{-8+0}{2},\\frac{0-2}{2})=(-4,-1).\n$$\n\nNow, to find the radius of the circle, we compute the distance between the center $(-4,-1)$ and a point on the circle, say $A(-8,0),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{(-4+8)}^{2}+(-1-0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{17}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, since the circle has center $(-4,-1)$ and radius $r=\\sqrt{17},$ the equation of the circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x-(-4))}^{2}+(y-(-1))^{2} & ={(\\sqrt{17})}^{2} \\\\ (x+4)^{2}+(y+1)^{2} & =17.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329104 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "603badf5-1f56-581f-833a-ec08fbedf777" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Circle Given a Line Covering a Diameter"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67763a38-60d4-5ce2-bfb1-77d3fba66510" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-4)^{2}+(y+6)^{2}=13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329106} {:answer/id #uuid "0bfaf101-903d-5e7f-9c9b-af5acb098a09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-4)^{2}+(y+6)^{2}=52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329107} {:answer/id #uuid "e4b7a98a-d153-5eb2-bd71-ca1abaeb61a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=2\\sqrt{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329108} {:answer/id #uuid "222bf662-e011-58d2-9a31-4c8b4450328f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329109} {:answer/id #uuid "5e2a6057-7c59-52a6-845f-800695f604fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329110}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0bfaf101-903d-5e7f-9c9b-af5acb098a09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-4)^{2}+(y+6)^{2}=52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329107} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ed40211-7a4a-5fdc-89f4-a0a73aa76dd0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329105}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ac7dcd31-21b4-5064-a3d7-5c55f9b204d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-117859" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/c100b27df3e4d9b23846961ad5d168fbe6f0eecd94ab8a9b99f51e82bca064a3.png)\n\nThe line $2y-3x=-24$ intercepts the $x$- and $y$-axes at points $A$ and $B$, respectively. If the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is a diameter of a circle, find the equation of the circle." :question/worked-solution "To find the $x$-coordinate of $A$, we set $y=0$ in the equation of the line and solve for $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(0)-3x & =-24 \\\\ -3x & =-24 \\\\ x & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, to find the $y$-coordinate of $B$, we set $x=0$ and solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y-3(0) & =-24 \\\\ 2y & =-24 \\\\ y & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $A$ and $B$ have the coordinates $(8,0)$ and $(0,-12)$, respectively.\n\nIf the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is a diameter of the circle, then the midpoint must be the center of the circle. Using the midpoint formula, we find that the center of the circle is at\n\n$$\n(\\frac{8+0}{2},\\frac{0-12}{2})=(4,-6).\n$$\n\nNow, to find the radius of the circle, we compute the distance between the center $(4,-6)$ and a point on the circle, say $A(8,0),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{(4-8)}^{2}+(-6-0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+36} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{52} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{13}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, since the circle has center $(4,-6)$ and radius $r=2\\sqrt{13},$ the equation of the circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4)^{2}+(y-(-6))^{2} & ={(2\\sqrt{13})}^{2} \\\\ (x-4)^{2}+(y+6)^{2} & =52.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329111 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e44305e5-b7e3-5ae2-925c-9329f37823b8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points of Intersection of a Circle with a Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e8de84de-b9d8-5e93-90ee-43c6ed8c923d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-2,2)$ and $(6,10)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329113} {:answer/id #uuid "ff23c307-f70c-56f9-99bb-4da66f8107a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-2,2)$ and $(5,9)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329114} {:answer/id #uuid "56e5ff07-edff-524d-ac7d-6b2441247823" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1.5,1.5)$ and $(5,10)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329115} {:answer/id #uuid "57f97ee3-8db2-5be9-ae63-04b75e095330" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,1)$ and $(5,9)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329116} {:answer/id #uuid "ca3ee1cd-f431-5899-992b-fcb78ec1d787" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,1)$ and $(6,10)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329117}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e8de84de-b9d8-5e93-90ee-43c6ed8c923d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-2,2)$ and $(6,10)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329113} :answer-field/id #uuid "54e92426-001f-5226-9eca-1501c36f8bd2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329112}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "249a4827-1fab-5d89-acce-30cbdc7d02ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-14665" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/8c9f433d2e23db9dc8abb81395e91acf59047e26221b1218393c4905548d1f35.png)\n\nFind the points where the circle $(x-3)^{2}+(y-5)^{2}=34$ intersects the line $y=x+4$ (sketched above)." :question/worked-solution "To find $x$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute $y=x+4$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2}+(y-5)^{2} & =34 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+(x+4-5)^{2} & =34 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+(x-1)^{2} & =34 \\\\ {x}^{2}-6x+9+{x}^{2}-2x+1 & =34 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-8x-24 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x-12 & =0 \\\\ (x-6)(x+2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute $x=-2,6$ into the equation of the line and compute $y$-values. Start with $x=-2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x+4 \\\\  & =-2+4 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first intersection point is $(-2,2).$ Next, we plug in $x=6$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x+4 \\\\  & =6+4 \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second intersection point is $(6,10).$\n\nTherefore, the line $y=x+4$ intersects the circle $(x-3)^{2}+(y-5)^{2}=34$ at the points $(-2,2)$ and $(6,10).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329118 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "603badf5-1f56-581f-833a-ec08fbedf777" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Circle Given a Line Covering a Diameter"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eada94d4-ae4c-52ab-a34c-861691c9edd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)^{2}+(y+2)^{2}=\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329120} {:answer/id #uuid "23d3947b-a3b6-5250-b92b-c40bc7923269" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}+(y+4)^{2}=\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329121} {:answer/id #uuid "30b95d83-3aa6-5d0b-a249-b1e0a9c2f7b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}+(y+2)^{2}=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329122} {:answer/id #uuid "4dc01482-028d-58f2-a3fa-ec8e6afea8f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}+(y+4)^{2}=10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329123} {:answer/id #uuid "5c757116-1504-5ffc-94d0-49f889371b78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)^{2}+(y+2)^{2}=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329124}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c757116-1504-5ffc-94d0-49f889371b78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)^{2}+(y+2)^{2}=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329124} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9d5e498-4274-57b8-a27d-0a83df00dfdd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329119}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "83e94835-de37-5d69-b0fd-aa884e963d7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-14670" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/d627d54450733af4faf74d55497064a4e6ffdc958601862cc49886988f7448c4.png)\n\nThe line $y=2x-4$ intercepts the $x$- and $y$-axes at points $A$ and $B$, respectively. If the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is a diameter of a circle, find the equation of the circle." :question/worked-solution "To find the $x$-coordinate of $A$, we set $y=0$ in the equation of the line and solve for $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-4 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =4 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, to find the $y$-coordinate of $B$, we set $x=0$ and solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(0)-4 & =y \\\\ y & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $A$ and $B$ have the coordinates $(2,0)$ and $(0,-4)$, respectively.\n\nIf the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is a diameter of the circle, then the midpoint must be the center of the circle. Using the midpoint formula, we find that the center of the circle is at\n\n$$\n(\\frac{2+0}{2},\\frac{0-4}{2})=(1,-2).\n$$\n\nNow, to find the radius of the circle, we compute the distance between the center $(1,-2)$ and a point on the circle, say $A(2,0),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{(1-2)}^{2}+(-2-0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-1)}^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, since the circle has center $(1,-2)$ and radius $r=\\sqrt{5},$ the equation of the circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x-1)}^{2}+(y-(-2))^{2} & ={(\\sqrt{5})}^{2} \\\\ {(x-1)}^{2}+(y+2)^{2} & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329125 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e44305e5-b7e3-5ae2-925c-9329f37823b8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points of Intersection of a Circle with a Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7186e710-e7d2-5f97-91b8-fc81553ade1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-4,-2)$ and $(1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329127} {:answer/id #uuid "74f35b7a-e710-5552-8eff-fdbd9fe09c8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-4,-2)$ and $(2,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329128} {:answer/id #uuid "2ca4f91f-7eb1-5ef4-8200-3295055974eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-3,-1)$ and $(1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329129} {:answer/id #uuid "ed6f3bfb-f40a-563e-8267-c86f1e878913" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-3,-1)$ and $(2,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329130} {:answer/id #uuid "a1099d2b-058c-5dfe-8647-be9cabb1413e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-4,-1)$ and $(2,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329131}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "74f35b7a-e710-5552-8eff-fdbd9fe09c8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-4,-2)$ and $(2,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329128} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fddc856-8795-517d-97c8-053c51c2231b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329126}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cfa05974-d33e-5f3c-90f3-bf0b329bfc3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-15805" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/de12c0b5be8318cc21c62615d24125998c22d55d37abafdee37c103c3840cb71.png)\n\nFind the points where the circle ${x}^{2}+(y+4)^{2}=20$ intersects the line $x-3y=2$ (sketched above)." :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation $x-3y=2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nTo find $x$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute $y=\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y+4)^{2} & =20 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{(\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}+4)}^{2} & =20 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{(\\frac{x}{3}+\\frac{10}{3})}^{2} & =20 \\\\ {x}^{2}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{20x}{9}+\\frac{100}{9} & =20 \\\\ \\frac{10{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{20x}{9}+\\frac{100}{9} & =20 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x+10 & =18 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x-8 & =0 \\\\ (x+4)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-4,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute $x=-4,2$ into the equation of the line and compute $y$-values. Start with $x=-4$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first intersection point is $(-4,-2).$ Next, we plug in $x=2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second intersection point is $(2,0).$\n\nTherefore, the line $x-3y=2$ intersects the circle ${x}^{2}+(y+4)^{2}=20$ at the points $(-4,-2)$ and $(2,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329132 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff002e26-f100-5b44-9175-a363d0808f9d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Single Point of Intersection of a Circle with a Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c968461-bf24-53a6-854e-2ed083bb1c8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329134} {:answer/id #uuid "8494e28e-c816-530d-a46e-8c2f51dd4277" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329135} {:answer/id #uuid "7610e9d2-4bfd-5e3b-8abf-8f5efebb4349" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329136} {:answer/id #uuid "37f9290c-5e0b-565b-b93d-d8acf994d262" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329137} {:answer/id #uuid "252af879-fed6-5806-95fd-54362544d1fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329138}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9c968461-bf24-53a6-854e-2ed083bb1c8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329134} :answer-field/id #uuid "b14fa81e-6252-56bd-ba2d-872f75be5089" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329133}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "df3cd888-e784-5638-9690-b7ff43d4a76a" :question/math-academy-id "q-15806" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/43fe48b1980d01aa9dbb7cc0f614272ad19e7cfe47b5a5a83a0f81e7539f285b.png)\n\nFind the points where the circle $(x-2)^{2}+(y+5)^{2}=10$ intersects the line $3y-x=-7.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation $3y-x=-7,$ as follows:\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{7}{3}\n$$\n\nTo find $x$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute $y=\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{7}{3}$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+(y+5)^{2} & =10 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+{(\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{7}{3}+5)}^{2} & =10 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+{(\\frac{x}{3}+\\frac{8}{3})}^{2} & =10 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x+4+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{16x}{9}+\\frac{64}{9} & =10 \\\\ \\frac{10{x}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{20x}{9}+\\frac{100}{9} & =10 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+10 & =9 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+1 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $y$-coordinate of the intersection point, we need to substitute $x=1$ into the equation of the line and compute $y$-value, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{7}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{7}{3} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the line $3y-x=-7$ intersects the circle $(x-2)^{2}+(y+5)^{2}=10$ at the point $(1,-2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329139 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d0e48e3-39ca-551a-80a4-dc7ff7855197" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying When a Circle Does Not Intersect with a Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e4f47b2e-c021-5309-a9c5-e845ed59476f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-3$ and $-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329141} {:answer/id #uuid "485296a9-ecec-55d5-a585-0bef558c41cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$ and $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329142} {:answer/id #uuid "e6bb7d67-b60d-5d5b-b126-db3d8e2a35f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The circle and the line do not intersect" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329143} {:answer/id #uuid "693b09a3-2021-5f03-82b7-612a6fe8cf87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-1$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329144} {:answer/id #uuid "a092a625-5df4-5dfa-886c-5cbda375a4ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329145}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e6bb7d67-b60d-5d5b-b126-db3d8e2a35f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The circle and the line do not intersect" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329143} :answer-field/id #uuid "20a20482-4a16-5f59-941c-b41061fd7257" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329140}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6de12d86-c297-540f-8fcc-949f18bd056f" :question/math-academy-id "q-54242" :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinates of the points where the circle $(x+1)^{2}+{y}^{2}=5$ intersects the line $y=-x+3.$" :question/worked-solution "To find $x$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute $y=-x+3$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x,$ if possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =5 \\\\ (x+1)^{2}+(-x+3)^{2} & =5 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x+1+{x}^{2}-6x+9 & =5 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-4x+5 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we compute the discriminant $D$ of the above quadratic equation, then we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =(-4)^{2}-4\\cdot 2\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =16-40 \\\\  & =-24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $D=-24<0$, there are no real solutions to the equation $2{x}^{2}-4x+5=0.$\n\nHence, we conclude that the circle $(x+1)^{2}+{y}^{2}=5$ does not intersect the line $y=-x+3$ at any point.\n\nThe corresponding diagram is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/85c9bea054f2e246d62864b022e53a6f1d23886b0f0c9f8c80c8e0a5b1be9cd8.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329146 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e44305e5-b7e3-5ae2-925c-9329f37823b8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points of Intersection of a Circle with a Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c143dc5-e2d5-5f65-9325-5fab419e19e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-2$ and $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329148} {:answer/id #uuid "a23e20ae-b0e6-51ad-a4d2-1ea5441d6419" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-3$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329149} {:answer/id #uuid "b58b6332-f33d-5467-a0b6-c7ac1869183e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-1.5$ and $2.5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329150} {:answer/id #uuid "da51350b-9f61-5aec-8cbd-ab28c1254987" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-2.5$ and $3.5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329151} {:answer/id #uuid "2e9e7741-5cb9-5871-b74c-8dc80d4c8b6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-2.5$ and $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329152}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a23e20ae-b0e6-51ad-a4d2-1ea5441d6419" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-3$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329149} :answer-field/id #uuid "123b99de-44e9-55a8-85c7-030b4d110157" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329147}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "245f8908-789b-540d-a8a8-808c244c83d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-7579" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/b29bf5d439b638bfe8ab3fd013467cab8ef7c393a1cd1cf06461fa5807b6c0ba.png)\n\nFind the $x$-coordinates of the points where the circle $(x-1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=25$ intersects the line $y=-x+1$ (sketched above)." :question/worked-solution "To find the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points, we substitute $y=-x+1$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =25 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+(-x+1-1)^{2} & =25 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+(-x)^{2} & =25 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+1+{x}^{2} & =25 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-2x-24 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-x-12 & =0 \\\\ (x+3)(x-4) & =0 \\\\ x & =-3,4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329153 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e44305e5-b7e3-5ae2-925c-9329f37823b8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points of Intersection of a Circle with a Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "767c078a-cb2b-50a8-9863-f6a9ec44a99c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2.5$ and $6.5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329155} {:answer/id #uuid "e16e11f9-5c15-508b-880c-49fd8092b8ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0.5$ and $4.5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329156} {:answer/id #uuid "dcd1d204-8c8d-54cd-9cae-7d3968da15bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ and $6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329157} {:answer/id #uuid "a4790855-b2ff-5fbd-89bc-bd5610e7584a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$ and $5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329158} {:answer/id #uuid "445238b3-cd2c-5c0b-9c79-d7b44681392c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1.5$ and $5.5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329159}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a4790855-b2ff-5fbd-89bc-bd5610e7584a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$ and $5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329158} :answer-field/id #uuid "bccae9b9-8a13-5be0-a550-44f1ab868ce4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329154}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bd4f4650-cbfe-54ae-b334-1592fbad61b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-7582" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/80646a56ea6df17b6f0b6460f3d4918174966773b9e7910d252c9098af3b0f48.png)\n\nFind the $x$-coordinates of the points where the circle $(x-4)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}=10$ intersects the line $y=x-3$ (sketched above)." :question/worked-solution "To find the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points, we substitute $y=x-3$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4)^{2}+(y+1)^{2} & =10 \\\\ (x-4)^{2}+(x-3+1)^{2} & =10 \\\\ (x-4)^{2}+(x-2)^{2} & =10 \\\\ {x}^{2}-8x+16+{x}^{2}-4x+4 & =10 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-12x+10 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-6x+5 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)(x-5) & =0 \\\\ x & =1,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088263 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff002e26-f100-5b44-9175-a363d0808f9d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Single Point of Intersection of a Circle with a Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f6ff98fd-61ee-57d1-9e4e-a9d278132a0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-6,-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086510} {:answer/id #uuid "0f0771a4-747a-5fc2-a12b-fe1be7d6a2ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086511} {:answer/id #uuid "2cfcd0f3-47b1-5888-abf8-5fd1b7010e69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086512} {:answer/id #uuid "37961cbc-c588-5c18-b1e6-7ac9e276b238" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086513} {:answer/id #uuid "4585f8f3-2eb6-5ca3-8422-3340193d0a1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086514}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4585f8f3-2eb6-5ca3-8422-3340193d0a1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086514} :answer-field/id #uuid "1bce527f-c6b8-5f80-92f1-6d59a200e59b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086509}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9e15ef62-40f8-5ebc-834d-a118abd6468a" :question/math-academy-id "q-7585" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/f17e4718742158f65db70bd25e0ba97fd0fba793b5212eee0ee60072eb4231e9.png)\n\nFind the point where the line $y=x$ intersects the circle $(x+4)^{2}+{y}^{2}=8.$" :question/worked-solution "To find $x$-coordinate of the intersection point, we need to substitute $y=x$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =8 \\\\ (x+4)^{2}+{x}^{2} & =8 \\\\ {x}^{2}+8x+16+{x}^{2} & =8 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+8x+8 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+4x+4 & =0 \\\\ (x+2)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x+2 & =0 \\\\ x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $y$-coordinate of the intersection point, we need to substitute $x=-2$ into the equation of the line and compute $y$-value, as follows:\n\n$$\ny=x=-2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the line $y=x$ intersects the circle $(x+4)^{2}+{y}^{2}=8$ at the point $(-2,-2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329160 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff002e26-f100-5b44-9175-a363d0808f9d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Single Point of Intersection of a Circle with a Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f65f1c7b-5b27-546f-988e-865db97af5c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329162} {:answer/id #uuid "2a1ac0fd-55af-5889-9e71-309f5a2df18d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329163} {:answer/id #uuid "0fe3383f-d918-5607-81c5-ae7e512a51bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(6,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329164} {:answer/id #uuid "b52026d1-8328-514b-90e9-7a1e871d6c3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329165} {:answer/id #uuid "21b7dd06-9298-5a34-8c2b-1b12c99bd369" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(6,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329166}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b52026d1-8328-514b-90e9-7a1e871d6c3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329165} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d991269-4eb3-5eba-8f30-af287cdbf0ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329161}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8c7d9d94-79b2-54cd-80f6-31a00c58749c" :question/math-academy-id "q-78512" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/d6f30f0e475fc222dc54c4e7f04bc3f48442b8430c3b830bfd7d11a5d6d788e3.png)\n\nFind the point where the line $y-2x=-11$ intersects the circle $(x-3)^{2}+{y}^{2}=5.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation $y-2x=-11,$ as follows: \n\n$$\ny=2x-11\n$$\n\nTo find $x$-coordinate of the intersection point, we need to substitute $y=2x-11$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =5 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+(2x-11)^{2} & =5 \\\\ {x}^{2}-6x+9+4{x}^{2}-44x+121 & =5 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}-50x+130 & =5 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}-50x+125 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-10x+25 & =0 \\\\ (x-5)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x-5 & =0 \\\\ x & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $y$-coordinate of the intersection point, we need to substitute $x=5$ into the equation of the line and compute $y$-value, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2x-11 \\\\  & =2(5)-11 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the line $y-2x=-11$ intersects the circle $(x-3)^{2}+{y}^{2}=5$ at the point $(5,-1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329167 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d0e48e3-39ca-551a-80a4-dc7ff7855197" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying When a Circle Does Not Intersect with a Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1046217e-9437-5289-8209-b079a3ed3e21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-4$ and $-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329169} {:answer/id #uuid "fc0c9670-1389-5aab-8bfb-da2fb7db4d95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-4$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329170} {:answer/id #uuid "b509a1e3-8b23-516a-853b-2cc4c5616ae0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The circle and the line do not intersect" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329171} {:answer/id #uuid "5496e9de-7ea6-5674-ba15-be502b995ef3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329172} {:answer/id #uuid "b999e9a8-1f8f-5099-a510-4b9555426023" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-1.5$ and $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329173}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b509a1e3-8b23-516a-853b-2cc4c5616ae0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The circle and the line do not intersect" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329171} :answer-field/id #uuid "62d5c5b0-c924-5b7f-8b0a-4854761b0596" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329168}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4e85f65e-e9ff-5ffd-af20-284e643c5763" :question/math-academy-id "q-78651" :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinates of the points where the circle $(x+4)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=4$ intersects the line $2x-y=-3.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation $2x-y=-3,$ as follows:\n\n$$\ny=2x+3\n$$\n\nTo find $x$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute $y=2x+3$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x$, if possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+4)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ (x+4)^{2}+(2x+3-2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ (x+4)^{2}+(2x+1)^{2} & =4 \\\\ {x}^{2}+8x+16+4{x}^{2}+4x+1 & =4 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+12x+13 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we compute the discriminant $D$ of the above quadratic equation, then we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={12}^{2}-4\\cdot 5\\cdot 13 \\\\  & =144-260 \\\\  & =-116.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $D=-116<0$, there are no real solutions to the equation $5{x}^{2}+12x+13=0.$\n\nHence, we conclude that the circle $(x+4)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=4$ does not intersect the line $2x-y=-3$ at any point.\n\nThe corresponding diagram is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/00bc159e9c25b913454c6037a822e41931c6224f3e6f0180da550fe9c857e718.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329174 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d0e48e3-39ca-551a-80a4-dc7ff7855197" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying When a Circle Does Not Intersect with a Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e0cbed9a-76fc-5516-abfa-21e54e5f6073" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329176} {:answer/id #uuid "42923d41-651f-5905-b4c1-3103c3bbc634" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-1$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329177} {:answer/id #uuid "32c1fbf7-f99c-513f-a695-fd26537f157c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-1$ and $-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329178} {:answer/id #uuid "a3df063b-9205-5f0e-b72b-66f776a24421" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The circle and the line do not intersect" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329179} {:answer/id #uuid "c5cfe886-10e4-5718-a993-c95f75626d46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329180}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3df063b-9205-5f0e-b72b-66f776a24421" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The circle and the line do not intersect" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329179} :answer-field/id #uuid "34bd55fe-b38d-5c67-92d4-c5e35befbbe1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329175}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "124f9d08-ccb9-500f-a740-588c701739e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-78654" :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinates of the points where the circle $(x-3)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=2$ intersects the line $y=2x+1.$" :question/worked-solution "To find $x$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute $y=2x+1$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x,$ if possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =2 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+(2x+1-2)^{2} & =2 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+(2x-1)^{2} & =2 \\\\ {x}^{2}-6x+9+4{x}^{2}-4x+1 & =2 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}-10x+8 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we compute the discriminant $D$ of the above quadratic equation, then we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =(-10)^{2}-4\\cdot 5\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =100-160 \\\\  & =-60.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $D=-60<0$, there are no real solutions to the equation $5{x}^{2}-10x+8=0.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the circle $(x-3)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=2$ does not intersect the line $y=2x+1.$ \n\nThe corresponding diagram is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/56e313584d7de2f1c40302135b00ef80a1d3c1e920407c9c4a0c62c3c6769456.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329181 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "603badf5-1f56-581f-833a-ec08fbedf777" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Circle Given a Line Covering a Diameter"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a306257d-786b-553b-862a-f324038468e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x-2)}^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329183} {:answer/id #uuid "33c247eb-27a8-5c0d-b61e-8dfc07ced165" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x-2)}^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329184} {:answer/id #uuid "f9cb0df4-6354-578a-b329-a87b54f96c61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x-2)}^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329185} {:answer/id #uuid "85730343-d77e-5bc8-9fd3-b80e643c2569" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x-1)}^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329186} {:answer/id #uuid "29bbf5c1-2ed7-5b9c-9cf4-70bf3f3a40c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x-1)}^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329187}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "29bbf5c1-2ed7-5b9c-9cf4-70bf3f3a40c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x-1)}^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329187} :answer-field/id #uuid "78b690eb-9273-5652-a783-e0dbc69058ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329182}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6a7d9a2f-2a4c-5379-aee8-ef76e2496ac3" :question/math-academy-id "q-78784" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/36e373800d2bd917e38bec197fbb5add2ae785270c68ddb4daf740ad678d4ece.png)\n\nThe line $x+y=2$ intercepts the $x$- and $y$-axes at points $A$ and $B$, respectively. If the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is a diameter of the circle, find the equation of the circle." :question/worked-solution "To find the $x$-coordinate of $A$, we set $y=0$ in the equation of the line and solve for $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+0 & =2 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, to find the $y$-coordinate of $B$, we set $x=0$ and solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+y & =2 \\\\ y & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $A$ and $B$ have the  coordinates $(2,0)$ and $(0,2)$, respectively.\n\nIf the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is a diameter of the circle, then the midpoint must be the center of the circle. Using the midpoint formula, we find that the center of the circle is at\n\n$$\n(\\frac{2+0}{2},\\frac{0+2}{2})=(1,1).\n$$\n\nNow, to find the radius of the circle, we compute the distance between the center $(1,1)$ and a point on the circle, say $A(2,0),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{(1-2)^{2}+(1-0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the circle has center $(1,1)$ and radius $r=\\sqrt{2},$ the equation of the circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x-1)}^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & ={(\\sqrt{2})}^{2} \\\\ {(x-1)}^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
